course: dictionary
lesson: 13654
title: Inflated: filled up, or made too big
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer wants. The word inflated can mean filled with air, or made bigger than is true or fair. «Ben blew until the red balloon was fully inflated.» The balloon is filled with air and has got bigger, so inflated means filled with air.
explain: «The shop had inflated prices, so a bag of crisps cost far more than it should.» Here the prices have been pushed higher than is fair, so inflated means made bigger or higher than it should be. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? Try "made too high" and see if it still makes sense.
explain: «Rafi had an inflated opinion of himself after one small win.» Nothing is being filled with air here. An opinion can grow too big, so inflated shows that Rafi thinks he is much better than he really is. The clue is in the words "of himself".
fact: Inflated can mean filled with air or gas, so that something gets bigger.
fact: «Inflated» can also mean made bigger, higher or more important than is true or fair.
fact: Inflation is when prices rise over time, so money buys less than it used to.
fact: Phrases to know: «inflated prices», «fully inflated», and «an inflated opinion of himself».
fact: To check the meaning, swap in a simple word such as "blown up", "too high" or "too big", and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The bouncy castle was fully inflated before the party started.» What does inflated mean here? | Very quiet | *Filled with air, so it is big and bouncy | Made of soft cloth | Lost somewhere in the garden
exercise: «The shop had inflated prices, so a bag of crisps cost far more than it should.» What does inflated suggest? | *Made higher than is fair | Given away for free | Made smaller | Filled with water
exercise: «Rafi had an inflated opinion of himself after one small win.» What does inflated show about Rafi? | *He thought he was better than he really was | He was very shy | He had a balloon at the party | He forgot his lunch
test: «The tyres were inflated with air at the petrol station.» What does inflated mean here? | Cold to touch | Flat and empty | *Filled with air | Painted red
test: «Her inflated claims about the team did not match the score.» What does inflated suggest? | Smaller than is true | Written in ink | Said in a whisper | *Made bigger than is true
exam: «Prices have inflated over the years, so bread costs more than it used to.» What does inflated mean here? | Filled with air like a balloon | Hidden under the table | Gone down to almost nothing | *Gone up, becoming higher than before
